# s4biz:site BEGIN — managed by deploy_direct.py. Edit THIS file, never the droplet's Caddyfile.
#
# The markers are the whole safety mechanism. The deploy deletes strictly between them and appends
# this file again, so it can never touch another project's block. A range delete keyed on a WORD
# instead ("/s4biz/,/^}/d") eventually starts inside somebody else's comment and truncates their
# site: that has happened on this exact host and produced a six hour outage.
s4biz.io {
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy s4biz-web:8000
}

# ONE CANONICAL HOST. www REDIRECTS, it does not serve.
#
# Both names used to serve the same bytes. That is duplicate content against our own canonical tag
# and sitemap, which both name the apex, and it made every monitor carry an alternation
# ("200 or 301 or 302 or 308") to describe a thing we had never actually decided. That alternation
# is what broke the uptime workflow: its fields are split on "|", the regex contained "|", and the
# expectation shredded itself into `^(200 and >= 301|302|308)$`. It then failed every ten minutes,
# for a site that was perfectly healthy, and mailed the operator each time.
#
# Deciding the behaviour removes the alternation, and with it the whole class of defect. Caddy
# still obtains a certificate for www, because this is a named site block.
www.s4biz.io {
	redir https://s4biz.io{uri} permanent
}
# s4biz:site END
